slstatus - suckless status
==========================
slstatus is a suckless status monitor for window managers that use WM_NAME
(e.g. dwm) or stdin to fill the status bar.


Features
--------
- Battery percentage/power/state
- CPU usage
- CPU frequency
- CPU iowait
- Custom shell commands
- Date and time
- Disk status (free storage, percentage, total storage and used storage)
- Available entropy
- Username/GID/UID
- Hostname
- IP address (IPv4 and IPv6)
- Kernel version
- Keyboard indicators
- Load average
- Number of files in a directory (hint: Maildir)
- Memory status (free memory, percentage, total memory and used memory)
- Swap status (free swap, percentage, total swap and used swap)
- Temperature
- Uptime
- Volume percentage (OSS/ALSA)
- WiFi signal percentage and ESSID
- Razer Basilisk X HyperSpeed DPI and battery status
- Claude and OpenAI subscription usage remaining
- Herdr agent states with an animated attention indicator
- Click controls for volume and mouse DPI when used with the clickable-status
  patch in this dwm configuration


Status clicks
-------------
- Volume: left click adds 10%, right click subtracts 10% (clamped to 0-100%)
- Mouse DPI: left click adds 100 DPI, right click subtracts 100 DPI (clamped
  to 100-16000 DPI)

The mouse component writes the active DPI and its single DPI stage together,
so the physical DPI button remains locked to the displayed value.


Requirements
------------
In order to build slstatus you need the Xlib header files and optionally ALSA
for volume percentage. PulseAudio is not supported for various reasons.

- I needed `pulseaudio` and `pusleaudio-alsa` in order to get my headphone volume controls working.


Installation
------------
Edit config.mk to match your local setup (slstatus is installed into the
/usr/local namespace by default).

Afterwards enter the following command to build and install slstatus (if
necessary as root):

    make clean install


Running slstatus
----------------
See the man page for details.


Configuration
-------------
slstatus can be customized by creating a custom config.h and (re)compiling the
source code. This keeps it fast, secure and simple.


Herdr agent status
------------------
The Herdr component reads the local Unix socket API directly and shows a count
for each agent state: `!` blocked, `✓` done, `…` working, `○` idle, and `?`
unknown. Zero counts are omitted.

Blocked and done agents need attention. While either state is present, the
component alternates `◆` and `◇` on each status refresh without changing the
width of the text. For example:

    Herdr ◆ !1 ✓1 …2
    Herdr ◇ !1 ✓1 …2

The component hides itself when Herdr is unavailable or has no detected
agents. Pass a socket path in `config.h` to override discovery. With a `NULL`
argument it checks `HERDR_SOCKET_PATH`, then
`$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/herdr/herdr.sock`, or finally
`$HOME/.config/herdr/herdr.sock`.


AI subscription usage
---------------------
The Claude and OpenAI components display remaining subscription capacity as
five-cell progress bars with the exact percentage alongside them, for example
`5hr [███▊░] 75%`. A fuller bar means more capacity remains. The components do
not use API keys or make network requests.

OpenAI usage is read from the newest Codex rollout under
`$CODEX_HOME/sessions` (or `~/.codex/sessions`). Codex writes the same rate-limit
snapshot used by its own interface after a response. Because the rollout format
is internal to Codex, an incompatible future format is treated as unavailable.

Claude Code exposes subscription rate limits to its custom status-line command
but does not persist them in its normal stats cache. Install this project's
helper and configure it as the Claude Code status line:

    "statusLine": {
        "type": "command",
        "command": "/usr/local/libexec/slstatus/claude-usage-cache"
    }

The helper requires `jq`, preserves the `[user@host directory]` Claude footer,
and writes only the two percentages and reset times to
`${XDG_CACHE_HOME:-$HOME/.cache}/slstatus/claude-usage-v1`.

Both components hide missing or expired windows. Values update only when the
corresponding CLI receives new usage data, so they can remain visible while
offline until the provider-reported reset time.
